---
version: 2.0.2
title: built-in needlessly-public-function: wording
file: ./test/grice/rules/needlessly_public_function_test.gleam
test_name: needlessly_public_function_wording_test
---
-- gleam.toml
target = "erlang"
name = "app"
-- src/app.gleam
@internal
pub fn helper() -> Int {
  1
}
-- src/app/internal/parser.gleam
pub fn parse() -> Int {
  1
}
-- output
warning[grice_rules/needlessly-public-function]: Public function no other Gleam module refers to
  ┌─ src/app.gleam:2:8
  │
2 │ pub fn helper() -> Int {
  │        ^^^^^^ no other Gleam module refers to `helper`

The public function `helper` is marked `@internal`, so it isn't part of the
package's public API, and no other Gleam module of the project refers to
it. Unless foreign code uses it, it doesn't need to be public.

Hint: If no foreign code uses it, make it private. If `app` doesn't use it
either, the compiler will then report it as unused.

warning[grice_rules/needlessly-public-function]: Public function no other Gleam module refers to
  ┌─ src/app/internal/parser.gleam:1:8
  │
1 │ pub fn parse() -> Int {
  │        ^^^^^ no other Gleam module refers to `parse`

The public function `parse` is in the internal module
`app/internal/parser`, so it isn't part of the package's public API, and no
other Gleam module of the project refers to it. Unless foreign code uses
it, it doesn't need to be public.

Hint: If no foreign code uses it, make it private. If `app/internal/parser`
doesn't use it either, the compiler will then report it as unused.

2 warnings in 2 modules.

